e (a reference, not the headline).** Var(τ̂) ≈ σ²L/n with L = Δxᵀ Σ̂⁻¹ Δx ≈ (1+d)/(p(1−p)); error grows with n⁻½, d^½ and the p(1−p) factor in noise-dominated cells. The *finding* is the departure: where σ²L/n over/under-states realised variance (hubs/BA, cluster designs, degree-correlated baselines, 2-hop features) and by what factor (effective n). *Evidence*: σ=0.25/0.5/1 slices, n slice (A2; without it no n-exponent exists), ER/SBM\n+**C1 — Variance follows leverage (a reference, not the headline).** Var(τ̂) ≈ σ²L/n with L = Δxᵀ Σ̂⁻¹ Δx ≈ (1+d)/(p(1−p)); error grows with n⁻½, d^½ and the p(1−p) factor in noise-dominated cells. The *finding* is the departure: where σ²L/n over/under-states realised variance (hubs/BA, cluster designs, degree-correlated baselines, 2-hop features) and by what factor (effective n). *Evidence*: σ=0.25/0.5/1 slices, n slice (A2; without it no n-exponent exists), ER/SBM\n-**C2 — Bias is set by curvature × corner distance, largely independent of d.** For a response curved in exposure, linear-fit spillover bias ≈ −½g''(1−2p) (+ third-order term); zero at p=½ to second order; for threshold responses with threshold outside the observed support the linear fit sees no spillover and the bias is the whole jump, independent of d in the limit of large d; at small d the support straddles the threshold and the bias is smaller or of opposite sign (`analysis` plan \n+**C2 — Bias is set by curvature × corner distance, largely independent of d.** For a response curved in exposure, linear-fit spillover bias ≈ −½g''(1−2p) (+ third-order term); zero at p=½ to second order; for threshold responses with threshold outside the observed support the linear fit sees no spillover and the bias is the whole jump, independent of d in the limit of large d; at small d the support straddles the threshold and the bias is smaller or of opposite sign (`analysis` plan \n-**C4 — Where regression wins, where DM or HT wins (the regime map; headline).** (Optional baselines Cortez 2022 and Yu 2022 are not listed as compared unless run on at least one cell. The one-shot K-step diffusion regression with ρ fitted, `or_fit` for (e), is reported under its own name as the oracle-form floor, never as message passing.) Cells where regression RMSE/DM RMSE < 1 vs > 1 as a function of spillover share s, mechanism, d, p; the rule \"regression beats DM below a threshold\" state\n+**C4 — Where regression wins, where DM or HT wins (the regime map; headline).** (Optional baselines Cortez 2022 and Yu 2022 are not listed as compared unless run on at least one cell. The one-shot K-step diffusion regression with ρ fitted, `or_fit` for (e), is reported under its own name as the oracle-form floor, never as message passing.) Cells where regression RMSE/DM RMSE < 1 vs > 1 as a function of spillover share s, mechanism, d, p; the rule \"regression beats DM below a threshold\" state\n-**C5 — Design interaction (graph-cluster randomization).** Cluster designs put exposures near 0/1 (smaller corner distance → less bias) but reduce effective n to ≈ number of clusters (more variance). Regression + cluster design beats both Bernoulli regression and cluster DM only in a band that is a function of resolution and realised cluster quality (modularity/conductance), not of the graph's name; detected communities are the realistic arm, SBM-truth clusters the oracle bound. Cluster b\n+**C5 — Design interaction (graph-cluster randomization).** Cluster designs put exposures near 0/1 (smaller corner distance → less bias) but reduce effective n to ≈ number of clusters (more variance). Regression + cluster design beats both Bernoulli regression and cluster DM only in a band that is a function of resolution and realised cluster quality (modularity/conductance), not of the graph's name; detected\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "483b2c66-fb97-56d0-9fb0-cfd2699754be", "at": 1795646008.50392, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 48078, "layer": "worker", "